// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row boxdyn_arg_deref_borrow_kills_box_drop (tier 1). PASSING
// `&b` FOR A `&dyn Tr` PARAMETER, WHERE `b` IS A `Box<dyn Tr>` LOCAL, DELETES
// THAT LOCAL'S SCOPE-EXIT DROP ENTIRELY: the payload's destructor never runs
// and the block is never freed.
//
// `use_ref(&b)` is the `&Box<dyn Tr>` -> `&dyn Tr` deref-coercion at an
// ARGUMENT position. Measured on build 19ff93338332ba46 43: 16 bytes
// definitely lost, destructor count 0 where 1 is correct, rc 1.
//
// CONTROLS ALREADY CORRECT, same binary, byte-for-byte the same program with
// ONE line changed:
//   * `b.v()` instead of `use_ref(&b)` — 0 bytes lost, count 1, rc 0. This is
//     the one-variable control: the borrow is the whole difference.
//   * `let b: Box<dyn Sp> = box_new(A{..});` with no borrow at all — clean.
//   * `let b: Box<dyn Sp> = make();` (return-position unsize coercion, the
//     other half of tests/spec/pass/coerce_box_dyn.logos) — clean on its own.
//   * `let r: &dyn Sp = &b; r.v();` — the SAME borrow in a `let` rather than an
//     argument — clean. SO THE BORROW IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATOR; THE ARGUMENT
//     POSITION IS, and a fix must not reach the `let` form.
//   * `Box<A>` (no `dyn`) cannot express the shape: `&Box<A>` does not coerce
//     to `&A` at all ("expected &A, got &Box<A>"), so the `dyn` half is not
//     incidental — it is the only place the coercion exists.
//
// ⚠ THE ORACLE IS A DESTRUCTOR COUNT, NOT AN EXIT CODE AND NOT valgrind.
// A leak reads as a clean exit in every other column in this tree, so `A`'s
// destructor adds to a counter reached through a raw `*mut i64`. Today the
// counter reads 0 and this program exits 1; correct is 1 and exit 0.
//
// Found by the 2026-09-11 valgrind re-sweep (sandbox/vg-round3). Corpus
// member, green through run_test.sh: tests/spec/pass/coerce_box_dyn.logos
// (8 bytes definitely lost at `logos_mem.logos.mem.boxed$box_new__g__T__A`,
// reached from `coerce_box_dyn$make`). Rust: exit 0, no leak.
package boxdyn_arg_deref_borrow_kills_box_drop;
use logos.mem.boxed;

trait Sp { fn v(&self) -> i64; }
struct A { x: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Sp for A { fn v(&self) -> i64 { return self.x; } }
impl Drop for A { fn drop(self: &mut A) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + 1i64; } } }

fn use_ref(r: &dyn Sp) -> i64 { return r.v(); }

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    {
        let b: Box<dyn Sp> = box_new(A { x: 42i64, c: p });
        if use_ref(&b) != 42i64 { return 3i32; }
    }
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    if got != 1i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
